<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Teraupoʻo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 1880s–<a href="/years/1897">1897</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Teraupo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Teraupoʻo was a chief of Raiatea in French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesia">Polynesia</a> who led armed resistance against French annexation of the island, proclaimed in 1888. His followers held out in the interior for nearly a decade, until a French expedition put down the rising and captured him in 1897, bringing Raiatea under full French control.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/teraupoo.jpg" alt="Teraupoʻo" width="320" height="452" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jules Agostini · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Raiatea_-_Le_Chef_Teraupoo_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Teraupoʻo was born around 1855 at Avera, a village on the east coast of Raiatea in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leeward-islands">Leeward Islands</a> of the Society Islands, under the name Hapaitahaa a Etau. He was counted a chief of a minor lineage. A decade before his birth the neighbouring Kingdom of Tahiti had been made a French protectorate in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-tahitian-war">Franco-Tahitian War</a>, while the Leeward kingdoms, Raiatea-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahaa">Tahaa</a> among them, had their independence guaranteed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jarnac-convention">Jarnac Convention</a> of 1847. The French historian Auguste Charles Eugène Caillot wrote that he came to resent the French after a French officer kicked him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1880 King Tahitoe of Raiatea accepted a provisional French protectorate and was deposed by his subjects for it. When King Tamatoa VI ordered submission to the French, Teraupoʻo refused, built up a resistance force in 1887 and set up a government at Avera under Tuarii, a younger daughter of Tahitoe, as queen. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> annexed Raiatea and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahaa">Tahaa</a> on 16 March 1888, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> agreed to set the 1847 convention aside. The French held the old capital of Uturoa, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> warfare kept them out of the countryside. His followers, the Teraupiste, were put at 800 men, though the historian Newbury counted hardly more than 359 under arms.</p><p>Teraupoʻo believed that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> would intervene, but the Raiateans appealed in vain to Robert Teesdale Simons, the British consul in Tahiti, and in 1895 the British Resident Frederick Moss refused to meet Queen Tuarii. In 1896 Governor Gustave Gallet brought two warships and two hundred soldiers from New Caledonia. The rebel government and 1700 rebels surrendered by his deadline of 1 January 1897, but Teraupoʻo fought on and hid with his wife and daughter in a cave on Faneuhi mountain until the light of a fire betrayed them on the night of 15–16 February 1897. The six-week campaign cost nearly fifty lives, mostly Raiatean.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The Chamber of Deputies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> ratified the annexation on 19 November 1897. The resistance leaders were deported to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noumea">Nouméa</a> in New Caledonia and their followers to Ua Huka in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marquesas-islands">Marquesas Islands</a>, while others were put to forced labour on Raiatea's roads. Teraupoʻo, his wife and his brother Hupe, who had served as his general-in-chief, remained in exile until 1905. Allowed to return, he lived as a recluse and died at Vaiaau on 23 December 1918, during the Spanish flu epidemic. The next indigenous leader to call for resistance to French rule was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pouvanaa-a-oopa">Pouvanaa a Oopa</a> in 1958.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Teraupo'o” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>France annexed Raiatea in 1888, but a resistance led by the chief <b>Teraupoʻo</b> lasted until 1897.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-raiatea">Kingdom of Raiatea</a></li></ul></section>
